      "Language": "jpn",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Indicators of ischemic heart disease were studied for the practice of preventive medicine."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Intravascular ultrasonography was performed in 97 patients with no abnormalities by left anterior descending artery angiography. Coronary risk factors were evaluated based on the relationship between plaque formation and lipoprotein levels."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Plaque was observed in 81% of patients. The relationship between mean plaque area(%) and the low-density lipoprotein/high-density lipoprotein(LDL/HDL)ratio was examined. Mean plaque area(%) was significantly higher at LDL/HDL < or = 2.5 than at LDL/HDL > 2.5. The border of significant change level of mean plaque area(%)was LDL/HDL = 2.5. There was a good correlation between mean plaque area (%) and LDL/HDL (r = 0.65, p < 0.01). Mean plaque area (%) at LDL/HDL 2.5 was calculated at 15%. Patients with coronary risk factors had LDL/HDL lower than 2.5 at mean plaque area of 15%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Maintaining LDL/HDL at 2.5 is very important for preventive medicine. However, LDL/HDL should be managed at under 2.5 in patients with coronary risk factors."
        }
      ]},
